section.p:
The registering of the user function we use the SaxonProcessor method registerUserFunction:

       void registerUserFunction(const char * ns, XdmValue * (*func)(int, XdmValue **),
           const char * signatureJSON)

p.samp:
XdmValue * myFunc(int length, XdmValue ** values) {
    SaxonProcessor * processor1 = new SaxonProcessor();
    XdmValue * myValue1 = values[0]->getHead();

    if(myValue1->isAtomic()) {
        XdmAtomicValue * item = (XdmAtomicValue *)(myValue1);
        long valuei = item->getLongValue() + 5;

        XdmAtomicValue * resultValue = processor1->makeLongValue(valuei);

        return (XdmValue *)resultValue;
    } else {
        if(values != NULL) {
               return (XdmValue *)values[0];
        }
        return NULL;
    }
}

section.p:
Calling the user extension functions from XSLT, XPath and XQuery is done first by
          declaring the namespace for the function. Then using the function name in your query or stylesheet.
          The arguments passed must match what is declared in the JSON signature.
          For example using the XPath API the following example calls the function:


    xpath->declareNamespace("dy", "http://www.example.com");
    XdmItem *result = xpath->evaluateSingle("dy:myFunc(3)");

Calling the user extension functions from XSLT, XPath and XQuery is done firstly by
          declaring the namespace for the function. Then using the function name in your query or stylesheet.
          The arguments passed must match what is declared in the JSON signature.
          For example using the XPath API the following example calls the function:


              $xpath->declareNamespace("dy", "http://www.example.com");
              $result = $xpath->evaluateSingle("dy:sumASequence([1,2,3,4,5])");
              if($result != null) {
              $value = $result->getAtomicValue()->getLongValue();
              echo 'Correct Result='.$value;

In the example above the PHP "sumASequence" extension function is called from XPath, but can be called
          from the XSLT stylesheet. The implementation of the sumASequence as PHP is given
          below. We make the assumption that the array contains numbers. This is a simple example but better error checking
          is required to make the code more robust. The returned integer is internally converted to an XdmAtomicValue:


          function sumASequence($arr){
          if(is_array($arr)){
            return array_sum($arr);
          }
          throw new Exception('$arr argument is not an array type');

          }

Calling the user extension functions from XSLT, XPath and XQuery is done firstly by
           declaring the namespace for the function. Then using the function name in your query or stylesheet.
           The arguments passed must match what is declared in the JSON signature.
           For example using the XPath API the following example calls the function:


               $xpath->declareNamespace("dy", "http://www.example.com");
               $result = $xpath->evaluateSingle("dy:sumASequence([1,2,3,4,5])");
               if($result != null) {
               $value = $result->getAtomicValue()->getLongValue();
               echo 'Correct Result='.$value;

In the example above the PHP "sumASequence" extension function is called from XPath, but can be called
           from the XSLT stylesheet. The implementation of the sumASequence as PHP is given
           below. We make the assumption that the array contains numbers. This is a simple example but better error checking
           is required to make the code more robust. The returned integer is internally converted to an XdmAtomicValue:


               function sumASequence($arr){
               if(is_array($arr)){
               return array_sum($arr);
               }
               throw new Exception('$arr argument is not an array type');

               }
